le in places like texas and arkansas  joined the confederate army in mass  many of the state regimental commanders  that i mentioned were  brigadier generals in the confederate  army greer groener  groener was a colonel but sems and some  of the others for example in texas of  the 30 castles  the captains of those castles 75 percent  joined the confederate army and there  were captains are higher  well the our area where i mentioned the  first defenders uh we also had castles  of the knights in berks and lehigh  county and our local paper did a feature  article on my book coming out but they  wanted a local connection so i knew they  had a castle and we went out and we  found in the history of lehigh county a  report of where the castle was located  it was north one mile north of linville  in lehigh county so we went out to try  to find it and my wife's a photographer  and this is a picture we took this was  the meeting place of that castle it was  it was the meeting place until the  unionists discovered and threatened to  blow the house up  and then they wisely moved the castle  meeting somewhere else but there was  another there's other reports of the  nights in foglesville in in berks county  where i was born and raised there were  nights at mooton and also at stouchburg  which is uh near wilmelsdorf uh west of  reading and during this during  april of 1863  three knights who were from that  stoutsburg castle were arrested in  reading and were taken to  philadelphia where they were tried for  conspiracy  so the knights were  active in pennsylvania and other parts  of the country  well in may of 1816 the knights  continued their threats against lincoln  in may of 1861  joseph holt was the judge advocate  general under lincoln and he was from  kentucky a strong unionist uh but he got  a letter which is in the library of  congress from his aunt and it said  joseph uh your nephew john has just  joined this group called the knights of  the golden circle and she found out they  took an oath to remove lincoln in his  cabinet  there were there were oaths that have  been posted in  ohio  that said i will not rest or sleep  until abraham lincoln now president  shall be removed out of the presidential  chair the knights that were captured in  berks county in 1863 one of the things  that they said in newspaper articles is  that they were they were hoping to  participate in the abduction of lincoln  lincoln regarded such threats from these  they called them dark lantern societies  with good-natured contempt he sort of  took a fatalistic attitude about it he  said they're going to get me they're  going to get me there's probably not  much i can do about it there was a  pigeon hole in his desk that had 80  letters 80 threat letters on his life  many of them undoubtedly coming from  members of the knights of the golden  circle one of the reasons that lincoln  refused to compromise in april of 1861  at the beginning of the civil war they  were trying to work a compromise called  the crittenden compromise proposed by a  senator from kentucky but lincoln said  if we compromise and we allow slavery  anywhere south of the missouri  compromise lines it's only going to  encourage groups filibustering groups  like the knights of the golden circle to  try to take over uh mexico cubans and  central america  now by 1864 the knights were being  subsumed by other super secret societies  once called the sons of of liberty but  they did become a bogeyman for the  republicans and were used very  effectively in the election of 1864 that  helped elect lincoln for example  before robert e lee's troops came into  gettysburg scam artists came in adams  county and sold the farmers insignias of  the knights of the golden circle and  said put these in your window and then  when lee's army swoops through they  won't hurt your farm  and so they did that they bought these  and they put them in the windows and uh  jubal early one of these lieutenants in  his memoir said we came into adams  county and they were waving these things  and we had no idea what they were so but  the joseph holt wrote a report on the  knights uh he really reported on the  later secret societies but he used the  knights sort of as the front the uh the  front part  in october of 1864 his report on the  secret society  as i mentioned john wilkes booth was a  night leader he worked with the knights  in memphis and other places in his  smuggling operations he sold he smuggled  quinine he was probably i mean his  sister asia talked about a meeting with  men in philadelphia strange men coming  in the middle of the night in late 1864  they were probably nights they were  whispering at all times of the nights  there are a suspicious number of nights  that were involved in the lincolnis  abduction first they tried to abduct  lincoln and take him to richmond to  exchange him for confederate prisoners  and then that failed when richmond was  evacuated and changed over into an uh  booth in the assassination but there  were knights who appeared to be involved  in both plots  there was booth obviously  the  for example  the uh  uh  the lachlan brothers i mentioned they  were both nights they're documented  nights lincoln stagehand edmund spangler  uh who held the door at ford's theater  uh is reputed to be a knight the  customer the actor  who was there doing suspicious things in  the united assassination named lewis  carlin was one of the gentlemen the  actors who joined with booth in  the summer of  during the knights of the golden circle  robert harris archer the colonel that i  mentioned uh who had the knight  certificate from harford county he was  picked up in a row boat  in the potomac river at the point when  booth and harold were expected to cross  and uh the feeling was that he was uh  trying to assist them in their escape uh  the confederate sky met spy man  spymaster from washington cornelius  spoil uh he was at he was in charge of  the confederate camp under lee at orange  courthouse virginia which is where booth  said he was going  and they were going to go to mexico and  that's where boyle went shortly  thereafter and there's actually in the  lincoln assassination a number at least  one  suspicious letter from a secret society  member to boyle and the sarats  uh basically saying that they're going  to kill lincoln chase and others i mean  the assassination plots were much  broader than lincoln and seward they  were talking about a whole slew they  were trying to wipe out the top of the  union government they were trying to  kill grant and sherman and so uh groups  like the knights of the golden circle i  was very initially very skeptical now  i'm becoming a believer believing that  they were  involved washington papers the day after  the assassination  said that the knights of the golden  circle were behind it  lincoln's special judge advocate general  who was brought in henry burnett to  review the assassination evidence he  reports that i find the footprints of  the knights of the golden circle  crossing my path in all directions and  this is a wood print from the library of  congress it shows  came out in late later in 1965 it shows  that the theory of assassination was  bickley the practice was booth and the  effect was the martyred president  well i submitted my draft for review to  lsu press in 2009 thinking i was almost  done  but with academic presses you get a  second reader who are usually anonymous  but are noted historians in the area and  i had historians reviewing my manuscript  asking questions i had to revise parts  and finally i got a second reader who i  later found out was dr robert may from  purdue university who was a noted author  and expert on the filibusters and dr may  had some very  favorable things to say about my book  actually the first blurb on the back is  from dr may  but among other things he said that the  book was  written and i tried to do this that the  text was written for the general reader  it was a  chronologically structured fast-paced  and clearly written narrative and i  tried to put the endnotes in the back  the material that the scholars would be  most interested in but in any event he  concluded his comments to lsu press by  saying you must publish this book  and they then went by  gave me a contract in 2011  i then discovered how there was still  more work to do i wanted to have  photographs in the book so i  included a photo gallery but i had to go  largely across the south the archives  and universities acquiring photos of  some of the nice leaders that you've  seen today  how to come up with a cover and they  accepted my idea this is a cover it's an  antique map showing the castle of the  knights of the golden circle and their  flag their their barrel flag as they  were getting ready to go into mexico in  april of 1861 which incidentally is  housed in the bikley files at the  national archives upstairs  we had to review the page proofs we had  to create an index uh develop a  publicity campaign my daughter allison  helped me develop my website  davidwww.davidkeen.com  where  lots posted if you want to take a look  at it  but  it brings us to where we are today  it took me much longer than i imagined  when i started out seven years  each chapter became a story unto itself  because each chapter covers the knights  in a different area of the country but  it was a fascinating journey with a  number of notable historical discoveries  i have shown that the knights were more  powerful than the historians have  previously assumed and played a more  central role in the outbreak of the  civil war  bickley in his abbreviated biography  which he put out in 1862 which is not  really just a paragraph he says i have  built up practical succession and  inaugurated the greatest war in history  and to a certain extent  he's right  thank  you maybe do we have any questions i  think if you come to the microphone  please don't do what i can answer was  there a connection between the knights  and the emergence of the ku klux klan  after the war  the uh  the knights died out one of the places  when they died out was in pulaski  tennessee and it was also where the ku  klux klan sprang up  uh in april uh  in 1866 in nathan bedford forest but i  have not been able to establish that  let me say one thing i didn't mention  was the fact even with the lincoln  assassination uh when i'm talking about  people who were knights it doesn't mean  the others weren't knights i mean  remember the knights were sort of  obligated to keep their identities  secret  and so when i've discovered that someone  was a knight it was usually from a lot  of digging a lot of detective work so  there's probable other members of the  lincoln assassination in nathan bedford  forest and others might have been  knights i just haven't been able to  establish that is there maybe that's  your next book um  question  were the nights uh significant in  southern illinois i've heard that they  were  yeah well the knights uh during the  early part of the civil war  were significant in southern illinois  southern ohio and southern indiana  they had castles there  but  by 1863  the castles were sort of switching over  to and bickley  got captured  by union elements most of the knights in  the south as a matter of fact  went into the southern army so in the  south they weren't all that effective  anymore because they basically gave  their allegiance to the southern army uh  in the in the border states and in the  midwest lower midwest they were active  but in in around 1863 they started to  switch over to another order called the  order of the american knights which was  out of st louis and several of the  members who were members of both said  that was because the knights of the  golden circle were by that time so  infiltrated by  union detectives and spies that there  was no longer you know they could no  longer communicate without knowing who  was who had infiltrated them so there's  new secret societies that sprang up but  during the early part of the civil war  yes they were active in those states  you said that uh breckenridge was a  knight and i believe you said all the  three southern governors were knights  but jefferson davis was not a knight or  what about his cabinet and vice  president and did  the knights were they able to  intimidate some of the state  legislatures and uh into uh secession as  a and you know try to put down the  unionist  the uh  the the the expositions that i showed  you also said that jefferson davis was a  knight i don't know whether he was or  not but i mean these were by anonymous  members so you don't know who's saying  it but they also said that he was a  knight  breckenridge is some evidence of that i  mean there were reports that he wore  night medallions when he was vice  president in  washington  and  there's also reports that crittenden  when he got back to kentucky accused  breckenridge of being a knight so i i  attached more credence to that and your  second question was what about the  intimidation of the state legislatures  in the south uh by the knights and  especially the intimidation of the  unionists  they were very effective i mean they uh  uh you know there was threats of  violence they looked through luggage uh  when northerners came down and this was  particularly places like texas and  piccolo admitted it you know when  northerners come here we look through  their the luggage uh we drive them out  they blame  uh northernest abolitionist preachers  for uh you know brainwashing the minds  of the children in the south against  slavery and so they did their best to to  kick them out interestingly in kentucky  however uh you know i mentioned began by  noting that they had tried to do that in  kentucky  and it was largely bickley at the time  because a lot of the other southern  leaders were already in the confederate  army bickley did a lot of bombasts he  did he's going to take over the capitol  in  it would take over the capitol in  frankfurt the knights were going to  march across the ohio river and split  the  northern ohio in half  those bombasts actually turned against  him and in kentucky uh lincoln sent in  lincoln guns he had his friends like  joseph speed one of his best friends at  speed brothers from louisville and they  actually uh joined together  and uh were  when when push came to shove  and the southern army entered  under pillow entered from tennessee and  grant came from cairo the legislature in  kentucky backed  grant the unionists and one of the  reasons for that is because i think the  reaction to all bickley's bombast so it  actually hurt them by that time but  earlier they were you know the militancy  was much more effective  dave in my own research i've come across  a group in philadelphia called the  knights of the blue gauntlet where there  are splinter groups from the knights of  the golden circle  they're they're during the civil war  they they use different names and i  can't i do know one night leader i think  he showed up in connecticut and he was  calling himself the knights of the  golden squares by that point but he had  the same he had the same documents of  the night so they used different names i  can't say  about the gauntlet but actually in  philadelphia this was last week i spoke  to the philadelphia union league the  civil war roundtables at the civil war  union league which had been formed  because of the southern sympathizers in  philadelphia in 1862 and bruce's sister  lived there asia and booth would come  there and you know southern sympathizers  would get together and the unionists in  philadelphia felt they needed a place  where they could talk in confidence and  that's why they founded the union league  there so uh but that particular group i  can't say for sure  anything else david we're out of time oh  okay thank you for coming all right well  thanks so much  the civil wa